# Store assets for {{APP_NAME}}

Everything the App Store and Google Play listings need, generated from `kickstart.store.json`
with no extra dependencies. Three steps: capture raw screenshots, render them into framed
marketing images, and hand the output to fastlane.

```sh
npm run store:shots            # = rn-kickstart-store render
npx rn-kickstart-store --help
```

## Files

| Path | What it is |
|---|---|
| `kickstart.store.json` | Devices, sizes, background, listing text and one entry per screen with localized copy. |
| `icon.png` | 1024x1024 master icon. Replace the placeholder before shipping. |
| `shots/<locale>/<id>.<ios\|android>.png` | Raw captures, one per screen entry and platform. |
| `../fastlane/screenshots/<locale>/` | Rendered App Store images, ready for `deliver`. |
| `../fastlane/metadata/<locale>/*.txt` | App Store listing text (`name`, `subtitle`, `description`, `keywords`). |
| `../fastlane/metadata/android/<locale>/images/` | Rendered Play images, feature graphic and icon, ready for `supply`. |
| `../fastlane/metadata/android/<locale>/*.txt` | Play listing text (`title`, `short_description`, `full_description`). |

## 1. Capture

Run the app on a simulator or emulator, then:

```sh
npx rn-kickstart-store capture                 # both platforms, prompts per screen
npx rn-kickstart-store capture --platform=ios --screen=home
```

On iOS this sets a clean status bar (`9:41`, full battery and signal) with `simctl status_bar`
and screenshots the booted simulator. On Android it enables SystemUI demo mode and uses
`adb exec-out screencap`. Each capture lands in `shots/<locale>/<id>.<platform>.png`. With
`--flow=.maestro/store.yaml` and Maestro on PATH, the flow runs instead and every
`takeScreenshot: <id>` step becomes that screen's shot.

A screen entry can also point at its own file with `"source": "path.png"`,
`{"ios": "...", "android": "..."}`, or a per-locale map of either.

## 2. Render

```sh
npx rn-kickstart-store check      # readiness: which shots are missing, store limits
npx rn-kickstart-store render     # every locale x size, written into fastlane/
npx rn-kickstart-store render --locale=en-US --size=iphone-6-9 --out=/tmp/preview
```

Each capture is drawn inside a geometric device frame on the configured background with the
template's look and the rhythm's layout per screen. Headlines support `*emphasis*` markup, which
gets a marker band in the `highlights` colour. Rendering uses headless Chrome, Chromium or Edge
(detected automatically). Only missing shots block a render; missing copy and store limits
(App Store 1-10, Play 2-8 tiles) are reported as advice.

Config knobs: `template` (`classic`, `notebook`, `editorial`), `rhythm` (`uniform`, `editorial`,
`showcase`, `magazine`, `storyboard`, `dynamic` or `template`), `device` / `androidDevice` /
`devices` (per size), `sizes`, `format` (`jpeg` or `png` for the App Store), `background`,
`settings` (any renderer setting: `textColor`, `highlights`, `frameColorId`, `headlineScale`...),
and per screen `overrides` of the same keys.

## 3. Icons

```sh
npx rn-kickstart-store icons --from=store/icon.png
```

Writes the iOS single-size asset catalog, Android legacy mipmaps, adaptive foreground and
background layers with `mipmap-anydpi-v26/ic_launcher.xml`, and the 512 px Play icon. Set
`iconBackground` in the config to pick the adaptive background colour; otherwise it is sampled
from the master's corner.

## 4. Upload with fastlane

The render step writes directly into the layout `deliver` and `supply` expect:

```sh
bundle exec fastlane deliver --skip-binary-upload
bundle exec fastlane supply --skip-upload-apk --skip-upload-aab
```

Add a locale by appending it to `locales`, adding `copy.<locale>` to each screen and a
`listing.<locale>` block, and capturing a `shots/<locale>/` set.
